{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794651579,"nonce":"R08OzWRG0ozG4o1Z2ip+kiJpAUU=","message":"Two findings for the frontend decision (Haxe 4.3.6, Reflaxe 4.0.0-beta, the Haxe unit suite).\n\nReflaxe cannot run on HashLink or hxcpp in any useful way.\n- `haxe.macro.Context` has no members outside a macro: `Context.defined(...)` fails to compile for --hl and for --cpp (\"has no field defined\").\n- Reflaxe's classes sit behind `#if (macro || reflaxe_runtime)`. With `-D reflaxe_runtime`, GenericCompiler does compile to a .hl. That mode is for IDE and null-safety checks: Reflaxe's own `reflaxe.helpers.Context` says so, and its non-macro branches return null or an empty array, with `TypedExpr` replaced by `Dynamic`. The typed AST (lazy `Ref`s into compiler memory) exists only inside the compiler's eval process, so a native build has nothing to compile.\n- std/eval has no foreign-function interface (NativeString, Vector, integers, luv, vm). A macro reaches Rust only through files, processes or sockets.\n\nSo Rust gets the AST through a hand-off, and the encoding of that hand-off is the cost. Zyntax's Reflaxe route had no byte encoding and went through JSON. Measured on the unit suite's typed AST (8902 bodies, 298616 nodes; tags, constants, locals and field names only, no types or positions):\n\n    macro side, binary writer (BytesOutput)     537 ms   1.33 MB\n    macro side, haxe.Json object tree           1384 ms  6.36 MB\n    macro side, hand-built JSON string          617 ms   6.35 MB\n    Rust, binary into node trees                8 ms\n    Rust, serde_json::Value                     83-98 ms\n\nThe binary file is 4.8x smaller and decodes about 10x faster. The macro-side win is against generic JSON (2.6x); a hand-built JSON string costs the same as binary. Typing the suite takes about 4 s either way, so the hand-off is not the dominant cost today, and types and positions will multiply the payload. Decision: option B with a compact binary format of our own, written by a thin exporter in eval, with all lowering in Rust. A socket to a running ash, so lowering overlaps typing, is a later option.","files":null}]}